<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mahavira</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">6th–5th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a> · Also known as Vardhamana</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mahavira, born Vardhamana, was the 24th and last tirthankara of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jainism</a>, a contemporary of the Buddha in the 6th or 5th century BCE. Born into a ruling clan near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaishali">Vaishali</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a>, he renounced his household at about thirty and, after years of austerity, taught a path of non-violence, truthfulness and non-possession. Jain tradition places his death at Pava.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mahavira.jpg" alt="Mahavira" width="320" height="485" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dayodaya · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mahavir.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Pratihara rulers were devotees of Vishnu, Shiva and the goddess Bhagavati, and Jain communities flourished under their rule: the <b>Mahavira</b> temple at Osian in Rajasthan is dated by a later inscription to the time of Vatsaraja.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gurjara-pratihara">Gurjara-Pratihara · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Siddhartha Gautama, the Buddha, attained enlightenment at Bodh Gaya in Magadha and preached at Rajagriha, where Bimbisara gave his order the Veluvana bamboo grove; <b>Vardhamana Mahavira</b>, the great teacher of Jainism, also taught in the region, and the Ajivikas of Makkhali Gosala were active there.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-magadha">Kingdom of Magadha · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Its closest contemporary rival was Jainism, taught by <b>Mahavira</b>, which shared much of its vocabulary and its rejection of animal sacrifice.</blockquote><a href="/religion/buddhism">Buddhism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>According to Jain tradition, twenty-four Tirthankaras have appeared in the present age of the world, beginning with Rishabhanatha, an immeasurably distant figure, and ending with <b>Mahavira</b>.</blockquote><a href="/religion/jainism">Jainism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>By tradition the Buddha attained awakening at Bodh Gaya, and by Jain tradition <b>Mahavira</b> was born near Vaishali; both taught in the kingdoms of the Ganges plain.</blockquote><a href="/essays/faiths-on-the-move">Faiths on the Move</a></li><li><blockquote>Buddhist texts describe how the Mallas of Kushinagar received the dying Buddha, cremated him and shared his relics, while Jain tradition places <b>Mahavira's</b> death at Pava.</blockquote><a href="/state/malla-republic">Malla republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Vaishali, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in Pawapuri, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=25.99,85.13&amp;year=-470" aria-label="Vaishali on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6800 1209 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Mahavira"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7250 1479h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7250 1479h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=25.99,85.13&amp;year=-470">What was here in 470 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
